of P&amp;L of minus $810.93. And the energy misses are part of why today's simulated hold is

00:08:38.356 --> 00:08:44.603
graded low before it even opens. You bought a rate-sensitive ETF the morning after a 20-basis

00:08:44.603 --> 00:08:51.023
point yield spike and expected the next federal open market committee, or FOMC, minutes to confirm

00:08:51.023 --> 00:08:57.223
a pause, yet the committee kept hiking because inflation was still above target. Worship's 2018

00:08:57.223 --> 00:09:03.483
comments on that exact episode show the mechanism. He argued that 10-year yields should track nominal GDP

00:09:03.483 --> 00:09:10.763
growth near 5.5%, and that a 20-basis point weekly move did not yet signal distress, treating the

00:09:10.763 --> 00:09:16.683
price action as information rather than a trigger for policy reversal. In a March 2016 speech,

00:09:16.863 --> 00:09:21.843
he faltered the Fed for a systematic underappreciation of contemporaneous market price signals,

00:09:21.843 --> 00:09:26.783
articles, typically followed by a belated and panicky policy response that validates market

00:09:26.783 --> 00:09:28.523
assumptions of a central bank put.

00:09:29.303 --> 00:09:34.683
In October 2018 on Jim Grant's podcast, he noted rising 10-year treasury yields would

00:09:34.683 --> 00:09:40.203
hit risk assets but did not sound concerned, calling the move nature and gravity rather

00:09:40.203 --> 00:09:41.243
than another tantrum.

00:09:41.863 --> 00:09:47.703
In a December 2018 Wall Street Journal op-ed with Stan Druckenmiller, he stated market indicators

00:09:47.703 --> 00:09:53.443
are not foolproof, but have a better track record than economists, and urge the Fed to cease for now

00:09:53.443 --> 00:09:58.763
its double-barreled blitz of higher interest rates and tighter liquidity. The pro habit is to pull

00:09:58.763 --> 00:10:03.923
the exact speech or op-ed from the prior cycle, note the inflation backdrop at the time,

00:10:04.363 --> 00:10:10.063
and compare it directly to today's consumer price index, or CPI, print before adjusting duration.

00:10:10.623 --> 00:10:15.423
The misconception to avoid is assuming every market dip will produce the same dovish response.

00:10:15.423 --> 00:10:21.183
The 2018 record shows the Fed only deferred when inflation was already at or below target.

00:10:21.703 --> 00:10:23.943
Remember, a dip is data, not a put.

